In 1938, the fossil hunter Roland T. Bird studied tracks along the Paluxy River near Glen Rose, Texas. One trackway showed a giant long-necked dinosaur walking along, with a large meat-eater following in its steps. Some scientists think it was a hunt. Others say the two may have passed at different times.

Bird dug up part of the trackway, and it is now on display at the American Museum of Natural History in New York. Elsewhere in Texas, he found tracks of about 20 long-necked dinosaurs travelling together, among the first evidence that some dinosaurs lived in herds.

{{story:fingerprints}}

Field seasons often happen in summer, when the weather is dry. Days start early to beat the heat. Teams carry water, hats, sunscreen, rock hammers, chisels, brushes, dental picks and glue to hold crumbly bones together.

Once a bone is found, the team digs a trench around it and builds a plaster jacket, like a cast on a broken arm. The jacket protects it on the bumpy ride back. Every bone's position is recorded, because how the skeleton lies in the rock tells a story about how the animal died and was buried.

{{story:fossils-form}}

For more than two thousand years, the Gulf of Mannar, between southern India and Sri Lanka, was one of the world's great pearl fisheries. Divers held their breath and sank to the sea bed with a stone for weight, filling baskets with oysters before shooting back up.

Greek and Roman writers mentioned the pearls of southern India. In the late 1200s, the traveller [[@marco-polo|Marco Polo]] described [[@diving-body|pearl divers]] off this coast on his way home from China. The port of Thoothukudi is still nicknamed the Pearl City. Far inland, [[@hyderabad|Hyderabad]] grew into a famous centre for drilling, sorting and selling pearls, a trade that continues in its old bazaars.

{{story:marco-polo}}

Chital are the deer most visitors see first. They have reddish-brown coats with neat white spots that they keep all their lives, unlike many deer whose spots fade. Herds of dozens graze in open forest clearings in parks like Kanha, Corbett and Bandipur.

Chital often feed beneath trees where langur monkeys sit. The monkeys drop leaves and fruit, which the deer eat. The monkeys watch from above, the deer sniff and listen below, and when one spots a tiger, both give alarm calls. Wildlife guides follow these calls to find big cats, which is why chital and [[@monkeys-langur|langurs]] are called a team.

{{story:national-parks}}

A mule is what you get when a male donkey and a female horse have a baby. Mules are often bigger and stronger than donkeys, but keep their steady temper and tough feet. From their mothers they get size and speed, much like [[@horses|horses]]. From their fathers they get patience, a careful way of placing each hoof, and the ability to work on less food. Horses have 64 [[chromosome|chromosomes]], donkeys 62, and mules end up with 63. Because the numbers don't pair up neatly, mules are almost always unable to have foals.

Armies have used mules for centuries to carry supplies in mountains. The Indian Army still keeps mules for high Himalayan posts where trucks can't go. In 2024 it also took delivery of 100 [[@robot-dogs|robotic "mules"]], four-legged machines that can carry loads and keep watch. In the USA, visitors have ridden mules down narrow trails into the Grand Canyon since the late 1800s. Mule trains still carry the post to Supai, a village of the Havasupai people deep in a side canyon that no road reaches.

{{story:robot-dogs}}

Army ants of the genus *Eciton* live in the [[@rainforests|rainforests]] of Central and South America. A single [[colony|colony]] can hold hundreds of thousands of ants, sometimes more than a million. Unlike most ants, they have no permanent nest. They march through the forest in long raiding columns, catching insects, spiders and other small animals.

Army ants are almost blind, and many kinds can't see at all. They follow each other by smell, laying chemical trails called [[pheromone|pheromones]] as they go. Speed matters. A raid can move about 20 metres an hour and sweep across a strip of forest floor as wide as a road. A gap in the path costs time, so plugging it with bodies is worth it, even though the ants in the bridge can't hunt.

{{story:algorithms}}

मध्यकालीन यूरोप में भिक्षु गिरजाघरों में लंबे-लंबे भजन गाते थे। शुरू में वे शब्दों के ऊपर छोटे-छोटे निशान लगाते थे, जो बस इशारा करते थे कि धुन ऊपर जा रही है या नीचे। गायकों को हर भजन ज़ुबानी याद करना पड़ता था, जिसमें सालों लगते थे।

लगभग 1000 ईसवी में गीदो दारेज़्ज़ो नाम के एक इतालवी भिक्षु को एक बेहतर तरकीब सूझी। उन्होंने आड़ी रेखाएँ खींचीं और सुरों को उन पर और उनके बीच रखा, ताकि सुर की ठीक-ठीक [[pitch|ऊँचाई]] साफ़ हो जाए। उन्होंने एक ऐसा भजन भी इस्तेमाल किया जिसकी पंक्तियाँ बढ़ते सुरों पर शुरू होती थीं, और जिनके पहले [[syllable|अक्षर]] थे *उत, रे, मी, फ़ा, सोल, ला*। गायक नए गीत कहीं ज़्यादा जल्दी सीखने लगे। बाद में 'उत' बन गया 'डो', और 'टी' जोड़ा गया।

{{story:printing-press}}

In August 1854, cholera swept through Soho in London, killing hundreds of people in a few days. Most doctors then believed diseases spread through bad air. A doctor named John Snow had a different idea: that cholera spread through dirty water.

He knocked on doors, asked where families drew their water and marked each death on a street map. The deaths clustered around a pump on Broad Street. Snow persuaded local officials to remove its handle. The outbreak was already fading, but his map became one of the most famous pieces of evidence in health history. It helped convince people that clean water could stop cholera.

{{story:maps-history}}

In the USA, counting has been the law since the very start. The [[@us-constitution|US Constitution]] says the people must be counted every ten years, and the first census, in 1790, found about 3.9 million people.

By 1880, the country had grown so much that adding up the answers by hand took about eight years. An engineer named Herman Hollerith had an idea. Each person's answers were punched as holes in a card. A machine pushed metal pins against the card, and wherever a pin passed through a hole, it closed an electric [[circuit|circuit]] and moved a counter. The 1890 census was tallied far faster. Hollerith's company later joined others to become IBM, and his punched cards fed some of the [[@first-computers|first computers]].

{{story:first-computers}}

People have kicked balls for thousands of years. FIFA recognises cuju, played in [[@ancient-china|ancient China]] more than 2,000 years ago, as the earliest known form. In [[medieval|medieval]] England whole villages played rough "mob football" through the streets.

In 1863, men from clubs and schools met at a tavern in London and wrote one [[@football-rules|set of rules]]. They called their organisation the Football Association. The game became "association football", shortened by students to "assoc", then "soccer". That is the name Americans still use, since their own [[@american-football|football]] is a different game.

{{story:kolkata}}

In 1900, Hollywood was a small farming town of a few hundred people, with lemon and orange groves. In 1910 it joined the city of Los Angeles, mainly to get a reliable water supply.

That same year, the director D. W. Griffith shot a short film called *In Old California* in the area. In 1911 the Nestor company opened Hollywood's first film studio, in an old roadside tavern on Sunset Boulevard. Within a few years, dozens of companies had followed, and the sleepy town was crowded with cowboys, cameras and people looking for work as extras.

{{story:sun-what}}

The Sulabh International Museum of Toilets opened in New Delhi in 1992. It was started by Bindeshwar Pathak, a social reformer whose organisation built cheap, clean public toilets across India.

The museum traces toilets over about 4,500 years. It begins with the drains and toilets of the [[@indus-cities|Indus cities]], among the earliest in the world, and moves through royal commodes, flushing toilets from Europe and designs for villages without sewers. The jokes come easily, but the purpose is serious. Clean toilets protect people from disease, and many Indian homes did not have one until recently.

{{story:toilets-sewers}}

Bobby Talyarkhan was a sports journalist with a rich voice and a talent for stories. His first broadcast in 1934 was of a match between the Parsees and the Muslims, part of a famous Bombay [[tournament|tournament]].

He didn't like sharing the microphone. He often spoke alone for a whole day's play, stopping only at lunch and tea. The historian Ramachandra Guha wrote about his flowing anecdotes. Listeners across India learned players' names through his voice. When All India Radio asked him to work in a team of three commentators in the late 1940s, he refused and left.

{{story:radio-india}}

In 2008 he captained India to win the Under-19 World Cup in Malaysia. Later that year he made his one-day debut. By 2011 he was part of the team that won the World Cup at home.

In one-day cricket, the team batting second must chase a target. Kohli became the master of this. He planned his innings like a sum, running hard between the wickets and hitting fours along the ground rather than risky sixes. On 15 November 2023 he hit his 50th ODI century, passing [[@sachin-tendulkar|Sachin Tendulkar]], and he finished that World Cup with 765 runs, a record for a single [[tournament|tournament]].

{{story:exercise}}

In 1900 a group of Greek sponge divers sheltered from a storm near the small island of Antikythera, between Crete and the Greek mainland. Diving, they found a shipwreck full of bronze and marble statues. Over the next year, they and the Greek navy brought up statues, pottery and coins.

{{story:shipwrecks}}

Among the finds was a shapeless lump of corroded bronze and wood. In 1902 an [[archaeologist|archaeologist]], Valerios Stais, noticed a gear wheel inside. At first, many people thought it must be a later object that had fallen into the wreck. Nothing like it was known from the ancient world.

Many websites used to demand a capital letter, a number and a symbol. People responded predictably: a capital at the start, a number at the end, and @ for a, 0 for o. Guessing programs learned these tricks and try them early.

In 2017, the US National Institute of Standards and Technology rewrote its advice. It now recommends long passwords, checking new passwords against lists of ones already stolen, and not forcing people to change them every few months. Britain's National Cyber Security Centre recommends using three random words.

{{story:cipher-codes}}

आंदोलन के बहुत से नेताओं ने [[@gandhi|गांधी]] को पढ़ा था, जिन्होंने अंग्रेज़ी राज के ख़िलाफ़ अहिंसक विरोध का रास्ता अपनाया था। विचार यह था कि अन्यायी क़ानून खुलेआम और शांति से तोड़ो, गिरफ़्तारी क़बूल करो, और कभी पलटकर हाथ मत उठाओ। इसे गांधी [[satyagraha|सत्याग्रह]] कहते थे। किंग इसके बारे में और जानने के लिए 1959 में भारत आए।

आंदोलनकारी इसके लिए बाक़ायदा अभ्यास करते थे। वे सीखते थे कि कोई चिल्लाए तब भी शांत कैसे रहें। विद्यार्थियों ने अलगाव वाले खाने के काउंटरों पर धरने दिए। 1963 में अलाबामा के बर्मिंघम में सैकड़ों स्कूली बच्चों ने जुलूस निकाला, और उनमें से बहुत से गिरफ़्तार हुए। पुलिस ने उन पर दमकल के पाइपों से पानी की तेज़ धार छोड़ी और कुत्ते छोड़ दिए। [[@television-invented|टेलीविज़न]] पर ये तस्वीरें देखकर बहुत से अमेरिकी आंदोलनकारियों के पक्ष में आ गए। "फ़्रीडम राइडर्स" बसों में बैठकर दक्षिण के राज्यों में घूमे, यह परखने के लिए कि सफ़र में अलगाव के ख़िलाफ़ बने क़ानून माने जा रहे हैं या नहीं।

{{story:supreme-courts}}

हम्पी का नज़ारा ऐसा लगता है मानो किसी दानव ने कंचों से भरा थैला उलट दिया हो। बड़े-बड़े गोल पत्थर पहाड़ियों की चोटियों पर टिके हैं, कभी-कभी तो बस एक नुकीले सिरे पर।

ये [[granite|ग्रेनाइट]] हैं, जो अरबों साल पहले ज़मीन के बहुत नीचे बने। बहुत लंबे समय में चट्टान टूटकर बड़े टुकड़ों में बँटी, और बारिश और हवा ने उनके कोने घिस-घिसकर उन्हें गोल कर दिया। इस घिसने को [[weathering|अपक्षय]] कहते हैं। विजयनगर के कारीगरों ने लगभग हर चीज़ में यही पत्थर लगाया, इसलिए पहाड़ियों की [[@rocks-types|चट्टानें]] और शहर की दीवारें एक ही हैं।

{{story:rocks-types}}

सेना के डॉक्टरों ने शेर आमी की जान बचाई और उसके लिए लकड़ी की एक छोटी-सी टाँग तराश दी। फ़्रांस ने उसे क्रोआ द गेर नाम का सैनिक तमगा दिया। उसे जहाज़ से अमेरिका भेजा गया, पर 1919 में अपने ज़ख़्मों की वजह से उसकी मौत हो गई।

उसके शरीर को सँभालकर रखा गया, और आज वह वॉशिंगटन के [[@smithsonian|स्मिथसोनियन]] नेशनल म्यूज़ियम ऑफ़ अमेरिकन हिस्ट्री में है।

{{story:pigeons-homing}}

The marine iguana is the only lizard in the world that feeds in the sea. It dives to graze on seaweed, then lies on black rocks to warm up in the sun. It sneezes salt out through its nose, which leaves white crusts on its head. It is one of the oddest [[@ocean-reptiles|ocean reptiles]] alive.

Galápagos [[@penguin-flight|penguins]] live farther north than any other [[@ocean-penguins-swim|penguins]]. Cold currents from the south and from deep water keep the sea around the western islands chilly enough for them. Nearby swim sea lions, and on land live flightless cormorants, whose wings have shrunk to stubs because they had no [[predator|predators]] to fly from.

{{story:invasive-species}}

Most people in Shillong are Khasi, one of Meghalaya's main communities, though many others live there too. The Khasi are matrilineal: children take their mother's clan name, and family property usually passes down the female line. The youngest daughter, the khadduh, traditionally [[inherit|inherits]] the ancestral house and cares for her parents.

This makes Khasi families different from most others in India. It's one of the many ways [[@families-world|families]] are organised around the world. Khasi men still play important roles in clan councils and community life. A mother's brother, the maternal uncle, has traditionally been an important guide for her children.

{{story:rock-music}}

The first alphabet grew out of Egyptian [[@hieroglyphs|hieroglyphs]], used by Semitic-speaking workers about 3,800 years ago. They took small pictures and used each for the first sound of its name. Aleph meant ox, and its letter was an ox head. Turn our capital A upside down and you can still see the horns.

Beth meant house, which gave us B. Gimel may have been a throwing stick or a camel, and it became the Greek gamma and our C and G. The Greeks kept the order but renamed the letters alpha, beta, gamma, delta. Put the first two together and you get the word "alphabet".

{{story:phoenicians}}

In 1905, a 27-year-old Bulgarian medical student named Stamen Grigorov was studying in Geneva. He looked at yogurt from his home country under a [[@microscopes|microscope]] and identified the rod-shaped bacterium that helps it set. It was named *Lactobacillus bulgaricus*.

{{story:microscopes}}

Around the same time, the Russian scientist Élie Metchnikoff noticed that some Bulgarian villagers lived long lives and suggested yogurt might be part of the reason. His idea made yogurt popular in Europe and America. Scientists still don't know whether yogurt helps people live longer.

Wheat was first farmed around 10,000 years ago in the Fertile Crescent, an arc of land across what is now Iraq, Syria and Turkey. It likes cooler, drier weather than rice. Ground into flour, it becomes [[@wheat-bread|bread]], rotis, pasta, [[@noodles-pasta|noodles]] and cakes.

China grows the most wheat, and India is second. In the 1960s, India was importing wheat to avoid [[famine|famine]]. New high-yield seeds, [[fertiliser|fertilisers]] and irrigation during the [[@green-revolution|Green Revolution]], especially in Punjab and Haryana, changed that within a few years. Kansas and North Dakota are among the big wheat states in the US.

{{story:farming-began}}
